  "question": "How do I return a 404 with a custom message?",
  "k": 5
}

Raise an `HTTPException` with `status_code=404` and your message in `detail`. To return HTTP responses with errors to the client you use `HTTPException`.[2]

```python
from fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException

app = FastAPI()

items = {"foo": "The Foo Wrestlers"}


@app.get("/items/{item_id}")
async def read_item(item_id: str):
    if item_id not in items:
        raise HTTPException(status_code=404, detail="Item not found")
    return {"item": items[item_id]}
```

Change the `detail` value to whatever message you want.

If you're serving a frontend and want a custom HTML page for missing paths instead, you can also serve a static `404.html` page for missing frontend paths[1] with `app.frontend("/", directory="dist", fallback="404.html")`. That response keeps a status code of `404`.[1] By default, `fallback` has a value of `fallback="auto"`. With this, if a `404.html` file is found, it will be used as the fallback automatically. So, you can normally omit the `fallback` argument.[1]
